{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CfXSLAxDW26TLcoW4xDGd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is structurally clean and idiomatic for this codebase:\n\n- **Logic is in the right unit.** The E.164 conversion rule lives entirely in the shared `TelephoneNormalizer`, and both `NormalizeOwnerTelephone` (store) and `RequireUniqueTelephone` (compare) route through the single `toE164` method — the \"cannot drift apart\" invariant the class documents is preserved. No rule logic leaked into the function classes.\n\n- **No duplication.** The comparison in `RequireUniqueTelephone` reuses `toE164` on both sides rather than reimplementing stripping, so owners stored outside the pipeline still compare correctly.\n\n- **The null-return / escalation split is good separation.** `toE164` stays a pure utility returning `null` for invalid input, and the OfficeFloor function class (`NormalizeOwnerTelephone`) is the only place that translates that into `InvalidTelephoneException`. The shared utility correctly stays free of the escalation type.\n\n- **Renames are coherent and complete.** `InvalidTelephoneException`'s field/getter moved from `normalized` to `telephone` (matching that it now carries the raw offending input, not a stripped value), and the constructor message, javadocs, handler javadoc, inline comments, and OpenAI spec description were all updated in step with the behavior. Nothing was left describing the old 10-digit rule.\n\n- **No catch-all growth.** Each class keeps its single responsibility and its one public method (per the OfficeFloor constraint), and the model annotation change (`@Digits`/10-digit `@Pattern` → E.164 `@Pattern`) is a clean in-place swap.\n\nA maintainer of this repo would find the result natural."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13489,"cache_read_input_toke